// What Thistlewood can carry, make, learn and say without a script.
//   item "id" name="..." blurb="..."                     something for the pockets
//   recipe "id" from="a b" makes="item" says="..." sound="..."   two items put together become a third
//   clue "id" page="evidence|photograph|testimony|note" title="..." text="..."   a casebook entry
//   remark "id" text="..."                                a stock line; {name} is the spot's name
// Items and clues are given by effects on spots, lines and choices (gives="hairpin", clue="new-plaque").

// ---------- items ----------

item "camera" name="The camera" blurb="A folding plate camera, mahogany and brass. The album is the reason I am here, the Raja believes. Press F to raise it."
item "candle" name="Candle stub and matches" blurb="Two inches of tallow and eleven matches. Press Q to light or snuff it. Light is how one is seen."
item "hairpin" name="A hairpin" blurb="A lady's hairpin, enamelled with a blue butterfly. Not mine. The bearer said this room was Mrs. Sahukar's until the guests were moved about. Nobody said why they were moved."
item "tripod-leg" name="A tripod leg" blurb="One brass leg of my tripod, fifty inches drawn out. The other two lean in the trunk like a pair of sulking cranes."
item "bolt-hook" name="A bolt hook" blurb="A hairpin bent into a hook and bound to a tripod leg with a bootlace. Fifty inches of ingenuity. My mother would have called it vandalism."

recipe "make-bolt-hook" from="hairpin tripod-leg" makes="bolt-hook" sound="bend" says="You bend the hairpin into a hook and lash it to the tip of the tripod leg with a bootlace. It is not elegant. It is long."

// ---------- clues ----------

clue "sudam-vanished" page="testimony" title="The Raja on his brother" text="Kumar Sudam, the Raja's half-brother, was taken by a man-eater on the night of the last full moon: the eleventh of September. He said it twice, the second time to the moon."
clue "bolted-in" page="note" title="The bolt" text="The guest wing is bolted from outside at night, \"because of the tiger.\" A tiger that is, I am told, already dead."
clue "the-man-eater" page="evidence" title="The man-eater" text="The tiger that took Kumar Sudam, mounted on a frame in the trophy gallery. Glass eyes, a snarl the taxidermist was proud of, and moth in the hide. Moth works slowly. Moth does not work in a month."
clue "photo-man-eater" page="photograph" title="Plate 1: the man-eater, close" text="The flash shows what the moonlight hid: bald patches, moth tracks in the fur, and on the underside of the hide a stencilled number: C.G. 29/4. Fresh tigers are not numbered."
clue "new-plaque" page="evidence" title="The plaque" text="\"The Man-eater of Chandragarh. Shot by His Highness, 16th September 1946. Avenged.\" The brass is bright as a new anna. Five days after Sudam vanished. The palace was very quick to avenge him."
clue "old-plaques" page="note" title="The roll of honour" text="Gaur, 1912. Sloth bear, 1919. Leopard, 1924. Wild boar, 1938, \"taken on foot by Kumar Sudam.\" The old Raja shot three of them; his other son shot the fourth, and was the only one named for it."
clue "taxidermy-book" page="evidence" title="The taxidermist's book" text="The old Raja's register of skins. \"29/4. Tigress, old. Died in the Mahanadi floods, 1929. Moth in the hide. Not fit for display: storeroom.\" The man-eater has been dead for seventeen years."
clue "dust-outline" page="evidence" title="A clean oblong" text="On the storeroom's rack of skins, a clean oblong in the dust where one hung until lately. The dust has had a month to begin again, no more."
clue "pulled-claws" page="evidence" title="The paw" text="A mounted tiger's paw on the workbench, with two claws missing. The sockets are pale and fresh, and a pair of pliers lies beside it with tiger hair still in the jaws. Somebody needed claws, and needed them without a tiger."
clue "photo-paw" page="photograph" title="Plate 2: the paw and the pliers" text="The paw, the empty sockets, the pliers. The flash made the glass eyes on the shelf above look up."
clue "cut-photograph" page="evidence" title="The shikar photograph" text="The hunting party of last month on the palace steps: the Raja, the Ghataks, the Sahukars, rifles and a dead chital. A sixth man has been cut out with scissors, neatly, leaving a man-shaped window onto the frame's velvet."

// ---------- Thistlewood's stock lines ----------

remark "wrong-item" text="No. Not with that. Not even I am that optimistic."
remark "nothing" text="{name}. I look at it. It does not look back. Good."
remark "photo-plain" text="A plate of {name}. The album will be grateful; the case will not."
remark "code-wrong" text="The dials turn, the handle doesn't. Somewhere a dog dreams of me."
remark "combine-no" text="Those two have nothing to say to each other."
// The controls, shown under the first night's title card.
remark "controls" text="WASD to walk, the mouse to look (click to hold it, or drag). E to touch, F to photograph, Q for the candle, Tab for the casebook, the right button to peer."
remark "controls-touch" text="Left thumb to walk, right thumb to look. The round button touches what you are looking at; the small ones photograph, light the candle and open the casebook."
remark "deduction-wrong" text="No. Something in that doesn't hold, and I would rather know which before I say it aloud. Again, slowly."

// ---------- Day 2 and Night 2 ----------

item "soot-cloth" name="A sooty handkerchief" blurb="My second-best handkerchief, black with lamp soot. For reading, apparently."
item "palm-leaf" name="A palm-leaf letter" blurb="A talapatra: a strip of dried palm leaf, incised with a stylus. Blank to the eye. The letters are cut, not written; they need something dark rubbed into them to be read."
item "read-letter" name="The old Raja's letter" blurb="The palm leaf, rubbed with soot, the letters standing out black: a father's letter to a son, in Odia, in the old Raja's own hand."
recipe "rub-the-leaf" from="palm-leaf soot-cloth" makes="read-letter" clue="malati-letter" says="You rub the soot into the leaf with your thumb, gently, the way the archive clerks in Calcutta do it. Letters come up out of the blank leaf like fish."

clue "ghatak-sitting" page="testimony" title="Mr. Ghatak, sitting" text="On the night of the last shikar \"we were all very honest.\" His wife: \"Pitambar was so honest he could not stand up.\" She would not face the lens; she kept turning towards Mr. Sahukar."
clue "sahukar-sitting" page="testimony" title="Mr. Sahukar, sitting" text="The forest lease had \"a sentimental difficulty, which has resolved itself.\" Mrs. Sahukar begged him not to speak about \"the driver.\" Nobody had mentioned the driver. She fainted on cue."
clue "kotwal-necessary" page="testimony" title="Kotwal the mynah" text="Asked to say good morning, the Raja's mynah said \"Do the necessary,\" in the Raja's own voice, breath and cough and all. The Raja laughed a beat too long."
clue "zenana-ghost" page="testimony" title="The gardener" text="The zenana has been empty twenty years. On moonlit nights the servants hear ankle bells on its roof: a dancer's ghost, waiting. The padlock on the zenana gate has been oiled this week."
clue "mirror-inlay" page="note" title="The brass in the floor" text="In the Sheesh Mahal, a line of brass runs from the south window past three mirror stands to the north wall. At the first stand it bends left; at the second it runs straight; at the third it bends right."
clue "wedding-fresco" page="evidence" title="The fresco in the beam" text="Lit only by a moonbeam off three mirrors: a wedding by a sacred fire. The old Raja, young; a dancer in a white sari with ankle bells. Under them, in Odia numerals, ୧୯୦୬: 1906."
clue "odia-numerals" page="note" title="The slate" text="Odia numerals, from a child's slate: ୧ 1, ୨ 2, ୩ 3, ୪ 4, ୫ 5, ୬ 6, ୭ 7, ୮ 8, ୯ 9, ୦ 0."
clue "numeral-shadow" page="evidence" title="The jaali's shadow" text="After half past one, the moon through the zenana court's great jaali writes three numerals on the marble: ୪ ୨ ୭. Four, two, seven."
clue "circled-zero" page="note" title="A numeral in a ring" text="Beside the jaali's three numerals, a fourth, ୦, in a ring of shadow, apart. As if it belonged to another lock, and somebody wanted it remembered."
clue "malati-met" page="testimony" title="Malati" text="Not a ghost: Sudam's mother, sixty, in white, living in the zenana since the old Raja died. She told Sudam last summer who his father was. He wanted only that the jungle never be sold, and told his brother so."
clue "malati-letter" page="evidence" title="The old Raja's letter" text="To my son Sudam, born of my lawful wife Malati, married by fire and by the registrar in the year 1906: you are my elder son. What is mine is yours. The papers are where the gifts are kept. Do not let your brother know before you must."

// ---------- Day 3 and Night 3 ----------

item "record-key" name="The record room key" blurb="An old iron key on a faded ribbon, from inside Mr. Gladstone's head."
item "dried-chilli" name="A dried chilli" blurb="A dried red chilli from the Dewan's tiffin. Small, wrinkled, and to a mynah, priceless."

clue "pugmarks" page="evidence" title="The pugmarks" text="At the stream: a tigress and two cubs, and the same tigress's marks a month old. The forest guard: \"A mother, sahib. She has never come near men.\""
clue "remains-place" page="testimony" title="Where they found him" text="The forest guard, at the clearing where Sudam's remains were found: \"No blood on the leaves. No drag marks. A tiger is not tidy. And the marks on him were all the same depth, like a comb.\""
clue "blaze-marks" page="evidence" title="The blazes on the sal" text="Hundreds of sal trees blazed and numbered in red for felling. Mr. Sahukar: \"I had them marked in August. Before the difficulty.\""
clue "dynamo-accounts" page="note" title="The electricity ledger" text="The Dewan's ledger of the dynamo's current. September, underlined twice in red: \"Larder circuit, night of the 11th-12th. One hour. Not authorised.\""
clue "forest-receipts" page="evidence" title="The forest receipts" text="June: from D. Sahukar, \"Advance on the Chandragarh sal lease, Rs. 40,000,\" received in the Raja's own hand. The lease is unsigned; the money is spent."
clue "unsigned-lease" page="evidence" title="The lease" text="A ninety-nine-year lease of the sal forest to D. Sahukar, stamped and ready. Unsigned on the line for the forest steward's consent. The forest steward was Kumar Sudam."
clue "sudam-refusal" page="evidence" title="Sudam's letter" text="\"Brother, I will not sign. Sell the jungle and you sell the tigress and the doe and the men who live by them. I want nothing of yours. I want only this.\" 1st September 1946."
clue "ball-values" page="note" title="The scoreboard" text="Red 1, yellow 2, green 3, brown 4, blue 5, pink 6, black 7."
clue "annotated-pamphlet" page="evidence" title="Mr. Ghatak's pamphlet" text="\"On the Medicinal Relapse,\" by P. Ghatak, privately printed. In the margins, in the Raja's hand, underlinings and notes: \"Ghatak will say it is philosophy.\" \"Sahukar needs only a reason.\" \"Lilabati will supply it.\""
clue "invitation-drafts" page="evidence" title="The invitation drafts" text="Four drafts of one invitation to a moonlight shikar, in the Raja's hand, the guest list crossed and recrossed until only the Ghataks and the Sahukars are left. Underlined: \"and S. to drive.\""
clue "kotwal-mimicry" page="testimony" title="Kotwal, in the dark" text="Fed a chilli, Kotwal gave the shikar night in its own voices: Mrs. Sahukar, \"Hold him, he touched me!\"; Mr. Ghatak, \"A relapse, gentlemen!\"; blows; Mr. Sahukar, \"Into the larder with him. With the pig.\" Then the Raja, low, at four in the morning: \"No, Bhima. Give me the key. I'll go myself.\""

// ---------- Day 4 and Night 4 ----------

item "kitchen-key" name="The kitchen key" blurb="A long iron key, left under my plate by a man who did not look back. Jagu's."
item "larder-key" name="The larder key" blurb="A heavy iron key from the bottom of the spice box, wrapped in a clean cloth, as if it had to be kept clean."
item "shovel" name="A shovel" blurb="The gardener's shovel. My mother would ask what an Inspector of Police wants with a shovel at three in the morning. So would I."

clue "jagu-proverb" page="testimony" title="Jagu" text="\"A guest who eats pakhala means to stay. A guest who means to stay should not ask questions in the kitchen.\" And: \"A kind guest should sleep at night, sahib. All night.\""
clue "alibis" page="testimony" title="The alibis" text="Mrs. Ghatak: \"We all went to bed at midnight. Pitambar says so. Dibakar says so.\" Mrs. Sahukar: \"I was in bed by ten.\" Kotwal remembers it otherwise, in all their voices."
clue "pancha-phutana" page="note" title="Pancha phutana" text="Jagu's board: into the hot oil, first mustard, then cumin, then fennel, then fenugreek, last nigella."
clue "menu-slate" page="evidence" title="The menu slate" text="\"11th Sept. Raja Sahib's guests. WILD BOAR, roasted whole in the garden. Pakhala. Kheer.\" Below, crossed out and written again: \"the pig from the larder.\""
clue "scratches" page="evidence" title="Inside the larder door" text="Scratches on the inside of the larder door at the height of a man's hands. Nails, not claws: long ones, then short ones, then very short ones. He was alive in there, and for a long time."
clue "coat-button" page="evidence" title="A button" text="A horn button from a man's coat, khaki thread still through it, in the crack of the larder floor. The forest steward wore khaki."
clue "silk-thread" page="photograph" title="Plate: the bolt" text="Caught on the edge of the larder bolt: a thread of crimson and gold silk. Not shikar clothes. Dinner clothes: a cummerbund."
clue "boar-tusks" page="evidence" title="The ash pit" text="Under a month's grey ash in the kitchen garden: bones, and a boar's jaw with both tusks. They roasted a pig."

// ---------- Day 5 and Night 5 ----------

item "drive-belt" name="The drive belt" blurb="A great loop of greasy leather, heavier than it looks, and it looks heavy."
item "wax-cylinder" name="A wax cylinder" blurb="A brown wax cylinder in a cardboard tube, labelled in a dead man's hand: \"Not to be played. C.S.D.\" The old Raja: Chandrasekhar Singh Deo."

clue "camera-manual" page="note" title="The Ensign manual" text="For portraits in strong light, a slow plate: a fast one fogs. For a large dark interior, a wide lens, stopped down, and a long exposure, with nobody moving."
clue "dynamo-lecture" page="testimony" title="The Dewan on the dynamo" text="\"First the steam valve, slowly. Then the drive belt onto the flywheel; the boys take it off at night. Then the starting lever. Always in that order.\" And: some fool ran the larder circuit for an hour last month, without authority."
clue "circuit-log" page="evidence" title="The circuit log" text="The dynamo house's log, 12th September 1946: \"04:10. Larder circuit ON. 05:05. OFF.\" Initialled not by the engineer but B.N.S.D.: Bhairab Narayan Singh Deo."
clue "cylinder-speed" page="note" title="The cylinder box" text="Every cylinder in the music room labelled in the same hand: \"Recorded at one hundred and sixty revolutions.\""
clue "confession" page="evidence" title="The old Raja's voice" text="\"I, Chandrasekhar Singh Deo, Raja of Chandragarh, set down that on the ninth of February 1906 I married Malati, dancer, by fire and before the registrar at Cuttack, and that our son Sudam is my eldest son and my heir. I have not the courage to say it aloud. I have made a machine say it for me.\""
clue "durbar-programme" page="note" title="The lantern programme" text="\"THE DELHI DURBAR, 1911, IN TWENTY-FOUR SLIDES. I. The Procession. II. The Elephants. III. The Pavilion. IV. The King-Emperor.\""
clue "slides-digits" page="evidence" title="The last slide" text="Scratched into the black border of the last durbar slide, in the old Raja's hand: ୧ ୯, \"the first two.\" One, nine. The first two of something."

// ---------- Day 6 and Night 6: the waterfall, the stepwell ----------
item "trunk-key" name="A small brass key" blurb="From the nail behind Bhima's lantern hook. Small enough to be a child's, or a trunk's."
clue "ghost-lecture" page="testimony" title="Mr. Ghatak on ghosts" text="\"Superstition is merely guilt in fancy dress, and I have nothing to feel guilty about.\" He would not walk back to the jeep alone, in daylight."
clue "lilabati-water" page="testimony" title="Mrs. Sahukar, by the water" text="\"I only said he touched me. I didn't say what to do. They did that.\" Then she fainted, efficiently."
clue "sluice-order" page="note" title="Bhima's sluice chart" text="\"To let the water down: upper first. Then lower. Then middle. Never the middle before the lower.\""
clue "tin-trunk" page="evidence" title="Bhima's exercise book" text="Forty years of the house's orders, in a watchman's hand. The last entry: \"12th Sept. Raja Sahib at breakfast: the rogue in the larder, do the necessary. Done. Nullah. Claws. Plaque ordered.\""
clue "claws-rifle" page="evidence" title="The bottom of the stepwell" text="In the silt where the water was: Sudam's rifle, S. carved in the stock, and in oilcloth two old tiger's claws, the two from the gallery paw."
clue "bhima-confession" page="testimony" title="Bhima" text="\"He was alive when they left. I know the marks of hands. At breakfast the Raja said: the rogue in the larder, do the necessary. I opened it. The boy was dead. I carried him to the nullah.\""

// ---------- Day 7 and Night 7: Kumar Purnima ----------
item "toshakhana-key" name="The toshakhana key" blurb="A long key with a tassel of faded gold thread, from the Raja's own key ring. Where the gifts are kept."
clue "pomfret-terms" page="testimony" title="Major Pomfret's terms" text="\"I cannot accuse a ruling prince on ghosts and mynah birds, Thistlewood. Paper. Bring me paper, and a case I can put in a letter to Delhi.\""
clue "raja-keys" page="note" title="The Raja's keys" text="While his valet dressed him the Raja hung his key ring on the stag's foot by his dressing mirror, and never once looked away from it."
clue "moondial-digit" page="evidence" title="The moondial" text="At the full moon's height the gnomon's shadow falls across the moondial's last line, and the brass there is engraved, very small: ୬. \"The last.\""
clue "torn-cummerbund" page="photograph" title="Plate: the cummerbund" text="In the Raja's dressing room, put away and not sent to the dhobi: a crimson-and-gold silk cummerbund, torn at the edge, threads pulled. The thread on the larder bolt came from here."
clue "marriage-certificate" page="evidence" title="The marriage certificate" text="Registrar, Cuttack, 9th February 1906: Chandrasekhar Singh Deo, Raja of Chandragarh, to Malati, by fire and by law. Witnessed. Stamped."
clue "old-raja-will" page="evidence" title="The old Raja's will" text="\"To my eldest son, Sudam, born of my lawful wife Malati, the gaddi of Chandragarh, the palace, and the sal forest entire, which is never to be sold.\" Signed, and sealed, and hidden."
